#{[17592186045463 #edb/ref 10 :activity.type/lesson #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045464 #edb/ref 10 :activity.type/review #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045485 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/easy #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045487 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/moderate #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045493 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/radio #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045498 #edb/ref 10 :answer.type/math #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1007 "Mathematical Methods for the Physical Sciences II" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1008 "Mathematical Methods for the Physical Sciences II continues the development of advanced mathematical techniques used throughout modern physics. Building on the analytical framework established in the first course, students study orthogonality, multivariable integration, vector calculus theorems, and advanced methods for differential equations.\nThe course emphasizes mathematical structures that arise naturally in physics, including inner product spaces, orthogonal projections, integral theorems of vector calculus, and transform methods for solving differential equations. These tools provide the mathematical foundation for analyzing electromagnetic fields, wave phenomena, dynamical systems, and statistical models encountered in advanced physics courses." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1009 "The course begins with the theory of projections in inner product spaces. Students study norms, inner products, orthogonality, and orthogonal complements in vector spaces over the real and complex numbers. Techniques such as orthogonal projections, the Gram–Schmidt process, and QR factorization allow vectors to be decomposed into orthogonal components. These ideas play a role in Fourier analysis, signal decomposition, and numerical methods used in physical simulations.\nStudents then explore quadratic forms and the diagonalization of symmetric matrices. The spectral theorem provides a geometric interpretation of symmetric transformations and leads to techniques for analyzing energy functions and stability in physical systems. Singular value decomposition is also introduced as a powerful matrix factorization with applications in numerical modeling and data analysis.\nApplications of linear algebra follow, including least-squares methods for solving overdetermined systems. These methods arise when fitting theoretical models to experimental data and when approximating solutions to physical equations.\nThe course then develops multivariable integration in three dimensions. Students compute triple integrals over general regions and apply coordinate transformations such as cylindrical and spherical coordinates. These techniques allow physicists to compute quantities such as mass distributions, charge densities, and gravitational or electric potentials.\nStudents next study line integrals and surface integrals of scalar and vector fields. Concepts such as circulation, flux, and path independence lead to the fundamental theorems of vector calculus, including Green’s theorem, the divergence theorem, and Stokes’ theorem. These results form the mathematical framework underlying conservation laws and the differential and integral formulations of electromagnetic theory.\nApplications of multivariable calculus follow, including vector mechanics and optimization. Students analyze motion using vector-valued functions, apply Newton’s second law to systems in the plane, and study work–energy relationships. Multiple integrals are used to compute mass, centers of mass, moments of inertia, and other physical quantities.\nThe course then returns to differential equations, extending earlier techniques to systems of linear differential equations. Students analyze dynamical systems using eigenvalues, phase portraits, and matrix methods, which appear in models of interacting systems such as coupled oscillators or predator–prey dynamics.\nLaplace transforms are introduced as a powerful tool for solving linear differential equations with discontinuous inputs or initial conditions. Boundary value problems and Fourier series are then developed, providing mathematical techniques used to solve differential equations describing heat flow, vibrating strings, wave propagation, and other physical phenomena.\nThe course concludes with numerical methods for differential equations and advanced probability topics. Students analyze the accuracy and stability of numerical schemes used in computational physics and study multivariate probability distributions and the central limit theorem, which appear in statistical physics and experimental analysis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1012 155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1013 "PS2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "24958065-b095-50f9-98ac-e7c4369816b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048424 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048424 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048424 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048424 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048424 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048424 #edb/ref 1031 "The Linear Correlation Coefficient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048424 #edb/ref 1033 1056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048424 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "9decebad-ba84-5d1a-9299-fa672cd17201" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049206 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049206 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049206 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049206 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049206 #edb/ref 1031 "The Correlation Coefficient for Two Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049206 #edb/ref 1033 3050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049206 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "e354e91d-75ea-5244-818d-ff4a2cb8189e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050280 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050280 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050280 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050280 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050280 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050280 #edb/ref 1031 "The Covariance Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050280 #edb/ref 1033 3939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050280 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "3c894a8e-e328-5ff1-a180-13f9317f8867" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050351 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050351 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050351 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050351 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050351 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050351 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050351 #edb/ref 1031 "The Covariance of Two Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050351 #edb/ref 1033 3049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050351 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "41e17e9e-452a-583f-8f59-6524e41f2803" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162140 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162140 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162140 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162140 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162140 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Correlation Coefficient Given Variances and Covariance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162140 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162140 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6b371f15-7541-5cd9-ac1c-e53fd3b8fc18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162141 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162141 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162141 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162141 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Correlation Coefficient of Two Discrete Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162141 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162141 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c5fab7dc-3857-5840-a349-6d90e5f6e280" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162142 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162142 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Correlation Coefficient of Two Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162142 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162142 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "51a3f265-d2dd-5e62-931e-4bf6d7597ea4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162188 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162188 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162188 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162188 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186163123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162188 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162188 #edb/ref 1045 "The Correlation Coefficient for Two Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162188 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186163010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162188 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162188 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4a097ed8-748d-54f4-a06d-fefa2b69e0d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162442 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162442 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146471" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162442 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162442 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=0.6,\\,\\text{E}[Y]=1,\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.4899,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.8944,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162442 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86b78d29-f123-58f2-82ad-24e8b4486f2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162442 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162442 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}xy\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0.1+0\\cdot 1\\cdot 0.1+0\\cdot 2\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & +1\\cdot 0\\cdot 0.3+1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0.1+1\\cdot 2\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =0.5-(0.6)(1) \\\\  & =-0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{-0.1}{0.4899\\cdot 0.8944} \\\\  & ≈0.23,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162446 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162446 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148368" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162446 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162446 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ | $0.7$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=0.3\\,\\text{E}[Y]=0.2,\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.4583,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.9798,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162446 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "972ce393-9eba-55fd-8649-cb51968b297d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162446 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162446 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}xy\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0.2+0\\cdot 1\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & +1\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0.2+1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =-0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =-0.1-(0.3)(0.2) \\\\  & =-0.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{-0.16}{0.4583\\cdot 0.9798} \\\\  & ≈-0.36,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163010 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163010 #edb/ref 1061 34624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163010 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5afd6705-a870-5fc5-8b7c-544c322a2815" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163010 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163121 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163121 #edb/ref 1061 39086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163121 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f053d13a-ebb8-5437-aa41-81a2459e4c6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163121 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163122 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163122 #edb/ref 1061 39089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163122 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba6783eb-c401-5b3d-aa3b-85965b1f3255" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163122 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163123 #edb/ref 1061 39123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163123 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "083a778d-72e3-562d-b4fc-a52938de9b8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163123 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163294 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two random variables with covariance $\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]$ and standard deviations $\\textrm {SD}[X]$ and $\\textrm {SD}[Y]$.\n\nThe **correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y$,** also known as the **Pearson correlation coefficient**, is denoted $\\rho$ (the Greek letter \"rho\") and is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]}{\\textrm {SD}[X]\\cdot \\textrm {SD}[Y]}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The correlation coefficient satisfies\n$-1\\leq \\rho \\leq 1$.\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then\n$\\rho = 0$.\nThe reverse implication does not always hold. In other words, $\\rho=0$ does *not* imply that $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n- Unlike the covariance, $\\rho$ is a *dimensionless* quantity. This means we'll get the same answer regardless of the units used to measure $X$ and $Y$.\n- Also unlike the covariance, $\\rho$ tells the strength of the correlation between $X$ and $Y$. We typically have the following rule of thumb:If $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\gt 0.7$, the correlation is **strong,** and the data generated by our two random variables closely follow a straight line.![](images/3f/3fd8b4e85bd76bbf78fdb2b135c49b3fd5e37fd9651dcae29d945d75aa45b905.png)If $0.3\\leq \\mid \\rho \\mid \\leq 0.7$, the correlation is **weak.** The data generated by our random variables follow a straight line, but many are far from the line.![](images/c1/c132ec90770bb84ad6c7dcc4b24773a923139c616f523ba0a23c1506c40c508c.png)If $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\lt 0.3$, then the correlation is **negligible.** No (linear) correlation exists between the data points generated by the random variables.![](images/9a/9afa80bfadc40b8b832b17dcea10d66d5a08955909d5bea66c7c010558b5ea19.png)\n\nLet's get some practice computing the correlation coefficient." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163294 #edb/ref 1065 5638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163294 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "96cc345f-656a-5faf-a2cf-47946504320d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163294 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416487 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186416507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416487 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186416508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416487 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186416509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416487 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416487 #edb/ref 1045 "The Correlation Coefficient for Two 